	"Auto-generated table of contents with active-section highlighting via IntersectionObserver. Server-renders from MDX getHeadings() or scans the DOM client-side.",
	"src/components/TableOfContents.astro",
	[100],
	"import Preview from \"@components/_docs/Preview.astro\";\nimport PropsTable from \"@components/_docs/PropsTable.astro\";\nimport TableOfContents from \"@components/TableOfContents.astro\";\n\n## Preview\n\n<Preview label=\"Live TOC scanning this page\" align=\"stretch\">\n  <div class=\"w-full max-w-xs not-prose\">\n    <TableOfContents selector=\"article\" minLevel={2} maxLevel={3} stickyOffset={0} />\n  </div>\n</Preview>\n\nA sticky in-page navigation panel for long documents. Two render modes:\n\n1. **Server-side** — pass headings from MDX's `getHeadings()`. The list is rendered during SSR (no flash, no DOM scan).\n2. **Client-side scan** — omit `headings` and provide a `selector`. The script reads headings from the DOM at page load.\n\nActive-section tracking is always client-side via `IntersectionObserver`, so the highlighted entry follows the reader as they scroll.\n\n## Server-side usage (recommended for MDX docs)\n\n```astro\n---\nimport { getEntry, render } from \"astro:content\";\nimport TableOfContents from \"@components/TableOfContents.astro\";\n\nconst entry = await getEntry(\"docs\", \"intro\");\nconst { Content, headings } = await render(entry);\n---\n\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-[1fr_16rem] gap-12\">\n  <article class=\"prose-doc\">\n    <Content />\n  </article>\n  <TableOfContents headings={headings} minLevel={2} maxLevel={3} />\n</div>\n```\n\n## Client-side scan\n\n```astro\n<TableOfContents selector=\"main\" minLevel={2} maxLevel={3} />\n```\n\nThe script auto-generates IDs on any heading that doesn't already have one (slugified from text content), so it works on plain HTML pages where rehype-slug isn't running.\n\n## Props\n\n<PropsTable props={[\n  { name: \"headings\", type: \"{ depth: number; slug: string; text: string }[]\", description: \"Pre-computed headings from MDX getHeadings(). When provided, the list renders during SSR.\" },\n  { name: \"selector\", type: \"string\", default: '\"main\"', description: \"CSS selector scanned client-side when `headings` is not provided.\" },\n  { name: \"minLevel\", type: \"number\", default: \"2\", description: \"Minimum heading level to include (1–6).\" },\n  { name: \"maxLevel\", type: \"number\", default: \"3\", description: \"Maximum heading level to include (1–6).\" },\n  { name: \"title\", type: \"string\", default: '\"On this page\"', description: \"Heading text above the list (and the nav aria-label).\" },\n  { name: \"stickyOffset\", type: \"number\", default: \"96\", description: \"Sticky position offset in pixels — accounts for fixed top nav.\" },\n  { name: \"class\", type: \"string\", description: \"Additional classes on the wrapper.\" },\n]} />\n\n## Mechanism\n\n### Active-section selection\n\nHeadings are observed with `IntersectionObserver` using a tight reading band (`rootMargin: \"-20% 0px -70% 0px\"`). When at least one heading is inside the band, the topmost one wins. When the band is empty (e.g. mid-content with no heading nearby), the script falls back to \"the last heading above the viewport top\" — so the active state never disappears mid-scroll.\n\n### Heading IDs\n\nIf a heading lacks an `id`, the script slugifies its text content and assigns one (with a `-2`, `-3` suffix on collisions). This is a fallback — for MDX-driven docs you'll typically get IDs from `rehype-slug` automatically, in which case the script just leaves them alone.\n\n### Sticky positioning\n\nThe component uses `position: sticky` with a `--toc-sticky-offset` CSS variable so it pins to the top of its container while scrolling, leaving room for any fixed page header.\n\n## Accessibility\n\n- `<aside>` element with a `<nav>` inside — both naturally announced by screen readers.\n- The `nav` carries `aria-label` from the `title` prop.\n- Active item gets `aria-current=\"true\"` (matches WAI-ARIA's authoring practice for current location in a navigation list).\n- Anchor jumps respect the project's `scroll-padding-top: 6rem` so the target heading lands below the fixed nav.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Multi-instance safe: each TOC reads its own selector / heading set.\n- Re-initialized on `astro:page-load` for view-transition navigation.\n- For very long pages, consider tightening to `maxLevel={2}` — `h3`s often produce a noisy list.",
